{"alignments":[],"candidates":[{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"2","reason":"The distance is |(-1)-(-11)|/√(3²+4²)=10/5=2.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"cn-math","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"cnm_h175_q1","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h171","cnm_008","cnm_075"],"id":"cnm_h175_q1_m1","label":"Apply the parallel-line distance formula","methodDirectNodeIds":{"cnm_h175_q1_m1":["cnm_h175"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h175","cnm_h171","cnm_008","cnm_075"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"2","reason":"The distance is |1-(-25)|/√(5²+12²)=26/13=2.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"cn-math","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"cnm_h175_q4","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h171","cnm_008","cnm_075"],"id":"cnm_h175_q4_m1","label":"Apply the parallel-line distance formula","methodDirectNodeIds":{"cnm_h175_q4_m1":["cnm_h175"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h175","cnm_h171","cnm_008","cnm_075"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":1,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D, 8a²/(a²+1)","reason":"The boundary lines are x+ay=±2a and ax-y=±a. The perpendicular pairs have separations 4a/√(1+a²) and 2a/√(1+a²). Multiplying gives the area 8a²/(1+a²), valid for every a>0.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2021_AMC_10A_Q24","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"parallel-line-distances","label":"Distances between parallel lines","methodDirectNodeIds":{"parallel-line-distances":["cnm_h175"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h175"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A. 6 square units","reason":"The base AB has length 3√2. The distance between x + y = 3 and x + y = 7 is 4/√2 = 2√2, so the area is (1/2)(3√2)(2√2) = 6.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2009_AMC_12B_Q09","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"parallel-line-height","label":"Find the base and parallel-line height","methodDirectNodeIds":{"parallel-line-height":["cnm_h173","cnm_h175","cnm_p148"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h173","cnm_h175","cnm_p148"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":4,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C, 7.6","reason":"The rectangle has area 10·8=80. The four corner triangles total 2(4·3/2)+2(6·5/2)=42, leaving parallelogram area 38. Since HE=√(4²+3²)=5, its corresponding height is d=38/5=7.6.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2004_AMC_8_Q24","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"parallel-line-distance","label":"Use coordinates and parallel-line separation","methodDirectNodeIds":{"parallel-line-distance":["cnm_084","cnm_h167","cnm_h169","cnm_h175"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_084","cnm_h167","cnm_h169","cnm_h175"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":4,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C (6.4)","reason":"Let SP have slope m, so PQ has slope -1/m. The opposite-side distances are 2|m|/sqrt(1+m^2) and 6/sqrt(1+m^2), giving |m|=3. First-quadrant placement selects m=3. The center lies on the midlines y=3(x-4) and y=-(x-10)/3, whose intersection is (23/5,9/5). Its coordinate sum is 32/5=6.4.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2012_AMC_12B_Q17","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"equal-parallel-line-distances","label":"Slopes and opposite-side distances","methodDirectNodeIds":{"equal-parallel-line-distances":["cnm_h168","cnm_h175","cnm_h172"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h168","cnm_h175","cnm_h172"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_083","cnm_h169","cnm_h171"],"id":"equal-opposite-side-distances","label":"Method 2: Equate the distances between opposite sides","methodDirectNodeIds":{"equal-opposite-side-distances":["cnm_191","cnm_084","cnm_h168","cnm_h175","cnm_h172"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_191","cnm_084","cnm_h168","cnm_h175","cnm_h172","cnm_083","cnm_h169","cnm_h171"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":5,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D (16)","reason":"Writing k=|a-b| gives (c-d)²=18k and (c+d)²=72k. Hence {c,d}={t,3t} and k=2t²/9. Integrality forces 3|t, so c+d≥12 and a+b≥k+2≥4. The tuple (1,3,3,9) attains both bounds, giving 16.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2007_AMC_12B_Q20","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"base-times-separation","label":"Parallel-line base and height","methodDirectNodeIds":{"base-times-separation":["cnm_h172","cnm_h173","cnm_h175","cnm_p147"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h172","cnm_h173","cnm_h175","cnm_p147"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":6,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A, y = (3/4)x + 1","reason":"Writing L as y = (3/4)x+b, its distance from the given line is |b-6|/√(1+9/16). Setting this equal to 4 gives |b-6| = 5, so b = 1 or 11. Only b = 1 is listed.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1963_AMC_12_Q33","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h168","cnm_065","cnm_008","cnm_075"],"id":"parallel-line-distance-formula","label":"Use the parallel-line distance formula","methodDirectNodeIds":{"parallel-line-distance-formula":["math:parallel-line-distance-loci","cnm_h175","cnm_h171"]},"nodeIds":["math:parallel-line-distance-loci","cnm_h175","cnm_h171","cnm_h168","cnm_065","cnm_008","cnm_075"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":6,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C. 2","reason":"If the parallel lines are distance d apart, points equidistant from them lie on their midparallel and have distance d/2 from each. The two lines at distance d/2 from l2 each meet that midparallel once, giving exactly two points.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc",